{"abstract":"On 20 October 2020, the Working Group “Exposure Models” of the Europe Regional Chapter of the International Society of Exposure Science (ISES Europe) organised an online workshop to discuss the theoretical background of models for the assessment of occupational exposure to chemicals. In this report, participants of the workshop with an active role before and during the workshop summarise the most relevant discussion points and conclusions of this well attended workshop. ISES Europe has identified exposure modelling as one priority area for the strategic development of exposure science in Europe in the coming years. This specific workshop aimed to discuss the main challenges in developing, validating, and using occupational-exposure models for regulatory purposes. The theoretical background, application domain, and limitations of different modelling approaches were presented and discussed, focusing on empirical “modifying-factor” or “mass-balance-based” approaches. During the discussions, these approaches were compared and analysed. Possibilities to address the discussed challenges could be a validation study involving alternative modelling approaches. The wider discussion touched upon the close relationship between modelling and monitoring and the need for better linkage of the methods and the need for common monitoring databases that include data on model parameters","author_count":15,"contributors":["Schlüter, Urs","Arnold, Susan","Borghi, Francesca","Cherrie, John","Fransman, Wouter","Heussen, Henri","Jayjock, Michael","Jensen, Keld Alstrup","Koivisto, Joonas","Koppisch, Dorothea","Meyer, Jessica","Spinazzè, Andrea","Tanarro, Celia","Verpaele, Steven","von Goetz, Natalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-782040"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Finland","Germany","Italy","Netherlands","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["ARCHE Consulting","All Global Affiliations","Cosanta BV, The Netherlands.","Department of Science and High Technology, University of Insubria, Como, Italy.","European Chemicals Agency","FOPH - Federal Office of Public Health, Berne, Switzerland.","Federal Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (BAuA), Dortmund, Germany.","Heriot-Watt University. Edinburgh Campus. UK","Institute for Occupational Safety and Health of the German Social Accident Insurance (IFA), Sankt Augustin, Germany.","Institute of Occupational Medicine, Edinburgh, Scotland, UK.","Jayjock Associates, LLC, Langhorne, PA, United States.","Netherlands Organisation for Applied Scientific Research (TNO), Utrecht, Netherlands.","Nickel Institute, Brussels, Belgium.","School of Public Health, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN, United States.","University of Helsinki, Institute for Atmospheric and Earth System Research (INAR), Helsinki, Finland."],"keyword":["Modelling","Occupational Exposure","Regulatory","mass balance modelling"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-OT"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"OT":["Air Pollution Management, Copenhagen, Denmark."]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"theoretical background of occupational exposure models report of an expert workshop of the ises europe working group exposure models","src":["nfa"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19031234","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-782040","identifier":[{"id":"85123031943","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["nfa-e8f797ec-21ff-4e88-8acb-912e4e247ea6"],"keyword":["Occupational exposure","mass-balance modelling","modelling","modyfying-factor modelling","regulatory","work-related exposure"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medic